{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-13_10-13-13-hashicorp_raft-real-run/draft && python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nupd={\n \\\"rationale\\\":\\\"Repair: the transfer receipt has five parsed events, so the decisive indices for its non-voter case are the admission, the transfer outcome and the recovery poll, not the numbers I had carried over from a later receipt; the growth receipt's indices were verified again against its own event list. No prose changes beyond the conditions wording.\\\",\n \\\"knowledge\\\":[\n  {\n   \\\"ref\\\":\\\"@addvoter-doc-promises-staging-but-installs-voter\\\",\n   \\\"kind\\\":\\\"fact\\\",\n   \\\"statement\\\":\\\"AddVoter's documentation still describes the deprecated staged behaviour ('will add the given server to the cluster as a staging server ... The leader will promote the staging server to a voter once that server is ready'), and BootstrapCluster's documentation recommends growing a cluster exactly that way (bootstrap one voter, then AddVoter the next server). The code does neither: appendConfigurationEntry installs the named server as a voter in configurations.latest and in the commitment map immediately, so in the documented growth step from one voter the surviving node's lease quorum and election quorum already require the new server. The caller-visible result is a successful AddVoter future, so the API reports success for a step that has just made the cluster unavailable until that server runs.\\\",\n   \\\"conditions\\\":\\\"Protocol version 3, single-voter configuration grown by one voter whose server is not running; witnessed by the cited execution, in which the configuration entry committed, the AddVoter future returned nil, the node left leadership and stayed a Candidate, and the cluster recovered only once the named server was started.\\\",\n   \\\"source_ids\\\":[\\\"addvoter-doc-staging-promise\\\",\\\"bootstrapcluster-growth-guidance\\\",\\\"appendconfiguration-installs-latest\\\",\\\"checkleaderlease-latest-quorum\\\"],\n   \\\"executions\\\":{\\\"e19ad6d076fa4d878b7bd0f90f067fca\\\":[8,9,10,11]}\n  },\n  {\n   \\\"ref\\\":\\\"@transfer-doc-allows-ineligible-target\\\",\n   \\\"kind\\\":\\\"fact\\\",\n   \\\"statement\\\":\\\"LeadershipTransferToServer's documentation offers to transfer leadership to 'a specific server in the cluster' and promises that a transfer which cannot complete 'will fail gracefully', while the code path accepts any server for which replication state exists, including a Nonvoter, and the protocol refuses to elect a non-voter candidate. Naming a non-voter therefore deposes the incumbent leader without transferring anything, and the future can still report success, so neither the documented eligibility restriction nor the documented graceful failure is what the caller receives.\\\",\n   \\\"conditions\\\":\\\"Protocol version 3, three voters plus a caught-up Nonvoter added through AddNonvoter; witnessed by the cited execution, in which the transfer future returned nil while the named target never became Leader and the incumbent stepped down.\\\",\n   \\\"source_ids\\\":[\\\"api-transfer-to-server\\\",\\\"initiate-transfer-check\\\",\\\"leaderloop-transfer-target\\\",\\\"electself-voters-only\\\",\\\"requestvote-highterm-stepdown-nonvoter-reject\\\"],\n   \\\"executions\\\":{\\\"79cf4ec11d694dfbaeb78ac8b07e1f67\\\":[2,3,4]}\n  }\n ]\n}\nopen('update.json','w').write(json.dumps(upd,indent=2)+\\\"\\\\n\\\")\nprint('ok', [(k['ref'], k['executions']) for k in json.load(open('update.json'))['knowledge']])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
